<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<section xml:id="chapter4_conditional-execution">
  <title>Conditional Execution</title>

  <p>
    Useful programs often need to make decisions. A
    <term>conditional statement</term> allows a program to execute code
    only when a particular condition is true. The simplest form is an
    <c>if</c> statement:
  </p>

  <program><![CDATA[
if (x > 0) {
    std::cout << "x is positive\n";
}
  ]]></program>

  <p>
    The expression inside the parentheses is called the
    <term>condition</term>. It is evaluated as either <c>true</c> or
    <c>false</c>. If the condition is true, the statements inside the
    braces are executed. If the condition is false, those statements
    are skipped and execution continues after the <c>if</c> statement.
  </p>

  <p>
    Conditions often use <term>comparison operators</term>. A comparison
    produces a Boolean value: either <c>true</c> or <c>false</c>.
  </p>

  <program><![CDATA[
x == y    // x is equal to y
x != y    // x is not equal to y
x > y     // x is greater than y
x < y     // x is less than y
x >= y    // x is greater than or equal to y
x <= y    // x is less than or equal to y
  ]]></program>

  <p>
    The C++ syntax for comparison differs from ordinary mathematical
    notation. In particular, <c>=</c> is the assignment operator, while
    <c>==</c> tests whether two values are equal. The operators for
    “less than or equal to” and “greater than or equal to” are
    <c>&lt;=</c> and <c>&gt;=</c>, not <c>=&lt;</c> or <c>=&gt;</c>.
  </p>

<warning>
  <p>
    Accidentally writing <c>=</c> instead of <c>==</c> in a condition
    may still produce valid C++ code. For example, <c>if (x = 5)</c>
    assigns 5 to <c>x</c>. The assignment expression has the value 5,
    which is nonzero and therefore interpreted as <c>true</c>.
    In general, zero is interpreted as <c>false</c>, while a nonzero
    value is interpreted as <c>true</c>.
  </p>
</warning>

  <listing label="conditional_execution_AC_1">
    <title>
      This program uses several independent <c>if</c> statements.
    </title>
    <program label="conditional_execution_AC_1_editor"
             interactive="activecode"
             line-numbers="yes">
      <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int x = 12;

    if (x == 12) {
        std::cout << "Equal!\n";
    }

    if (x != 13) {
        std::cout << "Not equal!\n";
    }

    if (x < 6) {
        std::cout << "Less than 6!\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>
  </listing>

  <p>
    Each <c>if</c> statement above is checked independently. Since
    <c>x</c> is 12, the first two conditions are true and their bodies
    execute. The condition <c>x &lt; 6</c> is false, so that body is
    skipped.
  </p>

  <exercise label="conditional_execution_1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <c>x</c> is 12. Which condition is true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x &lt; 6</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Compare 12 with 6 using the meaning of the less-than operator.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x == 13</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The equality operator tests whether the two values are the same.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><c>x != 13</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This comparison is true because the two values are different.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><c>x &gt; 20</c></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Compare the value of <c>x</c> with 20 before deciding whether
            this condition is true.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="conditional_execution_2">
    <statement>
      <p>Match each comparison operator with values that make it true.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback>
      <p>
        Read each comparison from left to right and compare the two values.
      </p>
    </feedback>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise order="1">x &gt; y</premise>
        <response>x = 10, y = 2</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="2">x &lt;= y</premise>
        <response>x = 5, y = 5</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="3">x &lt; y</premise>
        <response>x = 2, y = 10</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="conditional_execution_3">
    <statement>
      <p>Match each comparison operator with values that make it true.</p>
    </statement>
    <feedback>
      <p>
        Pay attention to whether equality is allowed by the operator.
      </p>
    </feedback>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise order="1">x == y</premise>
        <response>x = 3, y = 3</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="2">x &gt;= y</premise>
        <response>x = 6, y = 2</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise order="3">x &lt; y</premise>
        <response>x = 2, y = 6</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>
</section>
